# Product Management – Class-wise Learning Reflections

## Class 1 – Exploring the Role, Ownership and Accountability of a Product Manager (25 July 2026)

The first lecture was quite different from what I had expected. I initially assumed that the course would begin with discussions around Artificial Intelligence and its practical applications. However, the majority of the session was dedicated to understanding the responsibilities and expectations associated with the role of a Product Manager.

At the beginning, several students described a Product Manager as someone who acts as a bridge between business and technology teams, collects requirements and ensures coordination among different stakeholders. Our professor challenged these explanations and introduced a more fundamental aspect of the profession: **ownership**. He encouraged us to consider a Product Manager as the CEO of a product. This comparison gave me a completely different perspective on the position.

The cricket examples shared during the lecture made the idea of ownership easier to relate to. A cricket captain receives credit when the team wins, even if he has not contributed directly through batting. Similarly, an individual captain's excellent performance cannot compensate for the team's overall defeat. I understood that a Product Manager must take responsibility for the product's overall success or failure instead of shifting the blame to engineering, marketing or other departments whenever something does not go according to plan. Although accepting this level of responsibility can be demanding, it is an important characteristic of effective product leadership.

Another interesting discussion arose when a classmate questioned whether Product Management is essentially a responsibility where people rarely receive appreciation. I could relate to this question because similar experiences are not uncommon in corporate environments. Our professor explained that dissatisfaction and difficult situations are present in almost every professional and personal responsibility. However, this does not make the effort worthless. If someone continuously feels that their work is not valued, they should evaluate whether they are in the right role instead of allowing frustration to become a permanent part of their working life.

**My Key Takeaways:**

- **Ownership and Accountability:** Responsibilities can be divided among individuals and teams, but the Product Manager remains answerable for the overall results delivered by the product.
- **Influencing Without Direct Authority:** Product Managers frequently work with people who are not their direct reports. Therefore, the ability to build relationships, communicate effectively and influence decisions without formal authority is essential.
- **Understanding the Product Domain:** The role extends beyond communicating requirements between departments. A Product Manager must understand the product, its business environment and the people it serves to make informed decisions.

**My Personal Perspective:** I agree that ownership is fundamental to Product Management. However, I believe practising it can become complicated in large organisations. Product Managers may be expected to deliver specific outcomes while having limited control over engineering capacity, financial resources or organisational priorities. Therefore, accountability should ideally be supported by adequate authority and the freedom to make relevant decisions.

**How I Intend to Apply This Learning at Work:** In my current role, I have sometimes approached platform-related responsibilities mainly as coordination activities. Following this discussion, I want to pay greater attention to the final outcomes of the work rather than concentrating only on completing assigned tasks. I also intend to identify situations where I can take decisions independently instead of escalating matters unnecessarily. This should help me gradually move towards a more ownership-driven and product-focused approach.

