## Class 3 – Using Product Management Frameworks to Improve Decision-Making (18 August 2026)

The third lecture started with two classmates summarising important points from the earlier sessions. We then moved into an extensive discussion of various frameworks used in Product Management.

The frameworks covered included Jobs To Be Done, Double Diamond, Opportunity Solution Trees, Lean Canvas, Amazon's Working Backwards methodology, PR/FAQ, Porter's Five Forces, PESTLE, SWOT, Ansoff Matrix, Quality Function Deployment (QFD), RICE, Kano Model, MoSCoW, RACI/RAPID, one-way and two-way door decisions, and OODA.

Initially, the number of frameworks discussed seemed quite overwhelming. However, one statement from our professor helped bring clarity to the entire discussion: **frameworks are meant to assist decision-making, not replace independent thinking.**

He compared frameworks to the dials present in a vehicle rather than its steering wheel or braking system. I understood that frameworks provide useful information and direction, but they cannot make the actual decision on our behalf. Applying several frameworks to the same problem does not automatically improve the quality of the outcome. If an additional framework does not contribute new information, strengthen the available evidence or change the decision, using it may simply add unnecessary complexity.

A few concepts from this lecture particularly stayed with me:

- **Define the Customer Precisely:** Instead of referring to everyone as a generic user, we should identify the actual audience, such as a parent, farmer, driver or passenger. This helps us understand the specific problems faced by different customer groups and develop more meaningful personas.
- **Distinguish Transformation from Incremental Improvement:** The professor used the example of helping a caterpillar move faster versus enabling it to transform into a butterfly. This illustrated the difference between making an existing process more efficient and fundamentally changing what it can achieve. I realised that organisations sometimes describe relatively small improvements as transformations, even though the underlying experience remains largely unchanged.
- **Understand That Product Targets Keep Changing:** The professor compared product development to launching a missile towards a target that is constantly moving while competitors are also trying to reach it. This helped me understand why products require continuous monitoring and adjustments. Revising an earlier decision based on new information should be considered part of learning rather than an indication of failure.
- **Look at Scrum from a Product Management Perspective:** Since I have worked with Scrum methodologies for several years, I found this discussion particularly relevant. Our professor described a sprint as an agreement between the Product Manager and the engineering team. Once the sprint begins, the team should be allowed to work without frequent interruptions or continuous changes in scope. Priorities can be reviewed after the sprint concludes. Although cancelling a sprint is possible, repeatedly modifying its scope midway can affect the team's commitments. He also explained that shorter sprint cycles may be more suitable in environments where requirements change frequently, while longer cycles can work in relatively stable situations.

Towards the end, we also explored **value capture and product economics**. The discussion suggested that the organisation creating value may capture only around 15–25% of the overall value generated, with 20% being a reasonable assumption for planning purposes. The remaining benefits may be retained by customers and other participants in the ecosystem.

This made me realise that creating substantial value for customers does not necessarily translate into earning an equivalent amount of revenue. A Product Manager must understand how the benefits are distributed among different stakeholders and how the organisation can establish a sustainable business model around the value it creates.

**My Personal Reflection:** One of the most memorable parts of this lecture was unrelated to the frameworks. Our professor shared an experience of returning from leave and discovering that his organisation had shut down. Instead of concentrating only on his own difficulties, he immediately focused on supporting his team members in finding alternative opportunities.

I found this example particularly inspiring because it demonstrated an important aspect of leadership that goes beyond technical expertise or business knowledge. The way someone supports their colleagues during uncertain and difficult circumstances is equally important.

**How I Intend to Apply This Learning at Work:** I want to avoid selecting frameworks simply because they are popular or widely used. Instead, I will first understand the problem and then identify the approach that is most relevant to the situation. I also want to become more attentive to the actual customer segment, changing requirements and the financial value generated by the solutions I contribute to.

