## Class 6 – Expanding Technological Possibilities and Developing a Deeper Understanding of Customers (5 September 2026)

The lecture started with a few administrative discussions related to GitHub and project topic submissions. It then moved towards concepts that I found especially useful for developing a broader product mindset.

One of the major themes was understanding **the art of the possible**. Our professor explained that innovation is sometimes restricted not by the absence of technology but by our limited awareness of its capabilities.

For example, if I do not know that computer vision can recognise incorrectly labelled products, I may never think about developing a solution around that functionality. Similarly, audiobooks were previously expensive and difficult to produce at scale, but improvements in text-to-speech technology have made their creation considerably easier and more affordable.

This made me realise that discovering new possibilities can be an important starting point for product innovation.

The professor also shared an organisational example in which one department would print a document, obtain a physical signature, scan it and send it through email. The receiving department would then repeat the same steps. This illustrated how organisations sometimes continue using outdated processes even after the technological limitations that originally justified them have disappeared.

I understood that businesses should regularly review their established workflows and question whether certain activities are still necessary or are simply continuing because they have always been performed that way.

Another useful discussion involved preparing a **Product Requirements Document (PRD)**. Our professor recommended expressing requirements from the perspective of the actual stakeholder.

For example, a requirement could begin with a statement such as, "As a rider, I want..." This helps explain the problem and expected outcome from the stakeholder's point of view instead of directly describing the technical implementation.

A statement that a system should support 10,000 concurrent users, for instance, is a technical specification rather than a requirement expressed by a rider.

I also found the discussion about maintaining consistent terminology across teams important. If an organisation decides to use a particular term, such as rider, driver, right-giver or right-taker, that terminology should be applied consistently across engineering, customer support, product documentation and user manuals. Even minor differences in terminology can result in misunderstandings when multiple departments collaborate on the same product.

The concept of **Black Box Reframing** was another interesting topic. It involves retaining the existing inputs and outputs of a process while examining whether the internal activities can be redesigned using newer capabilities.

For example, an older process may have required 20 inputs, but a modern technology-based solution might achieve the same outcome using only 12. In another situation, we might combine three existing inputs with two additional inputs that were previously too expensive or difficult to obtain.

This approach encourages us to rethink the process itself rather than simply automate every existing step.

The second half of the lecture focused on **sentiment analysis**, which introduced me to a more detailed way of interpreting customer opinions.

Our professor explained that a basic positive or negative sentiment score, or a simple thumbs-up or thumbs-down response, may not provide sufficient information for meaningful product decisions.

He described an opinion through five important components:

1. **Object:** The product or entity being discussed.
2. **Feature:** The particular characteristic or aspect being evaluated.
3. **Sentiment Value:** Whether the expressed opinion is favourable, unfavourable or neutral.
4. **Opinion Holder:** The individual who is expressing the view.
5. **Time:** The point at which the opinion was shared.

The surrounding context also plays an important role. For instance, someone may appreciate a mobile phone's camera performance in daylight but complain about its quality at night. Both opinions concern the same feature, but the circumstances are different. A single customer review can also contain feedback about multiple product features.

One example from the lecture particularly caught my attention. A review contained the statement, "My mother was mad with me because I didn't tell her before I bought the phone."

Although this comment does not directly evaluate the phone's technical specifications, it provides insight into the emotional and personal circumstances surrounding the purchase.

Our professor explained that people generally do not experience the same emotional involvement when buying an ordinary item such as ice cream. This helped me understand that analysing feedback only through product features may overlook important information about customer behaviour and purchasing motivations.

Finally, we discussed **data consent and privacy**. Our professor emphasised that companies should act as responsible custodians of customer information rather than treating collected data as something they own without limitations.

We discussed India's Digital Personal Data Protection (DPDP) framework and GDPR, both of which are relevant when developing products that collect or process personal information. Consent, appropriate usage and responsible data handling must be considered from the beginning of product development.

**My Personal Reflection:** This lecture helped me understand that innovation does not always require inventing something completely new. It can also involve identifying capabilities that were previously unavailable, questioning outdated workflows and finding better ways to achieve existing outcomes.

The discussion about sentiment analysis was particularly valuable because it demonstrated that understanding the circumstances and emotions behind customer feedback can provide insights that a simple sentiment classification cannot capture.

I also developed a clearer understanding of the difference between stakeholder requirements and technical specifications. Maintaining this distinction can make communication between business and engineering teams considerably more effective.

