## Class 7 – Applying Product Management Principles to an Electricity Distribution Case Study (12 September 2026)

This was the last lecture before our midterm examination. Instead of following a conventional classroom format, our professor conducted almost the entire session through a practical business case. I found this approach effective because it allowed us to apply the concepts discussed in earlier lectures to an actual business scenario.

The case involved an organisation developing a customer-service solution for electricity distribution companies, commonly known as DISCOMs. Our role was to represent a company selling a solution to these organisations rather than acting as the electricity distribution provider itself.

As students suggested different strategies, our professor encouraged us to move beyond conventional textbook responses. For example, ideas related to premium pricing or brand positioning were not necessarily relevant to the purchasing priorities of a DISCOM. Such an organisation would primarily be interested in a solution that addresses a genuine operational challenge or improves its financial performance.

The discussion became particularly interesting when one of my classmates introduced **System Thinking**.

Electricity distribution companies purchase power in advance through forward contracts. If a particular area experiences a power outage, the organisation has already committed money towards electricity that it cannot temporarily distribute and sell to customers.

Therefore, reducing the time required to identify and resolve an outage is not simply a customer-service improvement. It can also help the organisation reduce financial losses and protect its margins.

This changed my understanding of the product's value proposition. Instead of positioning the solution as a platform for improving call-centre operations, we could explain how it contributes to faster fault detection, shorter outages and reduced financial impact.

One of the most thought-provoking questions raised during the case discussion was: **Why should customers need to report an outage at all?**

If the system can monitor electricity consumption across different localities, an unusual decline in consumption may indicate a possible disruption. This information could allow the DISCOM to identify an outage proactively instead of waiting for customers to contact its support centre.

Even when a customer calls, the organisation may already possess relevant details such as their registered phone number, address and electricity meter information. If the customer is not at home, GPS information could potentially help identify their current location and the issue affecting them.

With these capabilities, the system could prevent a significant number of unnecessary customer calls.

I found this to be a strong example of the difference between **making an existing process more efficient and fundamentally redesigning it**. Improving the performance of a call centre is one possible solution, but eliminating the need for certain calls altogether represents a more significant change.

The pricing discussion introduced another important business consideration. An outcome-based pricing structure, where the provider charges for each successfully resolved ticket, connects the payment directly with the results delivered. However, this also transfers some operational risk to the solution provider because the number of tickets can fluctuate considerably depending on the frequency of outages.

We also discussed seat-based pricing. This model can create conflicting incentives because a customer that improves its operational efficiency may require fewer support agents. As a result, the provider could experience a reduction in revenue even though its product has delivered better results.

I understood that pricing structures should be designed to support sustainable revenue while ensuring that the interests of the customer and provider remain aligned.

Another framework introduced during this case was **TFIR – Truth, Facts, Inferences and Recommendations**.

It helped me understand the importance of distinguishing between different types of information within an AI-based product.

- **Truth and Facts:** These should be retrieved from dependable sources, such as databases and verified systems, rather than relying entirely on AI-generated responses.
- **Inferences:** These represent conclusions derived from available information and should be supported by relevant evidence.
- **Recommendations:** This is the most sensitive category because the system moves beyond presenting information and starts suggesting possible actions. Recommendations should expose their supporting information and reasoning so that humans can identify incorrect assumptions or unreliable conclusions.

We also covered several additional topics:

- **Third-Party Dependencies and Supply-Chain Risks:** Build, buy and subscription decisions involve more than financial considerations. If an OTP service provider experiences an outage, internet banking services may become unavailable. Similarly, a change or discontinuation of an external AI model can affect a live product even if the organisation has not modified its own application code.
- **Continuous Monitoring After AI Product Launch:** In conventional software, uncertainty generally reduces considerably after General Availability (GA). However, AI products may continue experiencing changes because their external models, APIs and dependencies can evolve independently.
- **Product Managers Should Understand Their Own Solutions:** A Product Manager should be capable of demonstrating the product independently. Constantly depending on another team member to explain or demonstrate the solution may indicate insufficient product understanding.
- **Maintain Regular Interaction With Customer Support:** Spending time with support teams after launch helps Product Managers identify recurring customer issues, usability challenges and missing capabilities that may not have been apparent during development.

**My Personal Reflection:** The most valuable part of this case was understanding how a product's value proposition can change when we examine the complete business system instead of concentrating on one department's immediate requirements.

I realised that a solution that initially appears to be a customer-support improvement platform could potentially become a tool for reducing operational losses and preventing customer complaints. This reinforced the importance of understanding the larger business environment before defining product requirements.

