## Class 8 – Progressing from Prototypes to Scalable Products Through Evidence-Based Validation (3 October 2026)

This was our first lecture after the midterm examination. The discussion started with a topic that I had already encountered in my professional work but had not previously understood in such a structured manner: **Provider Instability**.

Our professor explained two different forms of uncertainty that can affect AI-based applications.

The first is the normal probabilistic behaviour of AI models. For example, if a model generates incorrect responses approximately 5% of the time, its performance may remain around that level when evaluated under similar conditions.

The second is instability caused by changes made by external providers. A vendor may modify something internally, resulting in different outputs even when the prompt, testing environment and application remain unchanged.

Our professor shared an example in which an AI agent was instructed to continue working towards a particular objective but stopped after completing only one step. He related this to a Kannada saying about a donkey that moves forward only one step after every beating.

Although the comparison was humorous, it highlighted a serious challenge. During development, teams can investigate unexpected behaviour and reverse changes. However, production environments are more complicated because unreliable responses may already have reached customers before the issue is detected.

Another concept that I found particularly useful was **Assumption Drift**.

Our professor described an example in which an AI system was instructed to test a process involving three banks. The system interpreted this as a permanent requirement that exactly three banks would always be present. When the scenario was later changed to include only one bank, the system failed because it had silently incorporated an assumption that was never explicitly stated.

This demonstrated how easily AI systems can introduce hidden assumptions into their reasoning. Such assumptions may go unnoticed when Product Managers review generated results superficially and approve them without checking the underlying logic.

The professor also explained that Product Managers can become decision-making bottlenecks when every activity requires their approval. Under pressure, they may approve suggestions simply by responding with "fine, go ahead", allowing incorrect assumptions to move into the product.

The main subject of the lecture was the progression through different stages of product development:

**Concept → Prototype → MVP → Pilot → Scale**

The important principle was that teams should not automatically move to the next stage just because the previous activity has been completed. They must demonstrate that the current stage has achieved its intended objectives.

A prototype is mainly developed to explore an idea, demonstrate its potential and gather initial learning. An MVP, however, should provide sufficient functionality for actual customers to use it and offer meaningful feedback.

We also explored different forms of prototypes, including concept prototypes, user-interface prototypes, data prototypes and workflow prototypes.

The discussion about **Wizard of Oz MVPs** was especially interesting. This approach involves using a human behind the scenes to perform activities that customers may believe are being handled by an automated system.

Our professor made an important distinction between using human assistance and concealing that assistance from customers. Having a human involved during early product validation is acceptable, but intentionally misleading customers about how the service works is not.

I understood that teams can use manual processes to test customer demand and validate workflows before investing in complete automation. However, transparency about the service being provided must be maintained.

Several practical lessons from this session were:

- **Avoid Excessive Dependence on a Single AI Provider:** The product architecture should allow changes in external model providers without requiring extensive modifications. Since providers may expose different APIs and capabilities, unnecessary coupling should be avoided.
- **Establish Evaluation Parameters in Advance:** Every prototype or MVP should have a clear brief explaining its objective, scope and expected results. This prevents misunderstandings when different team members interpret success differently.
- **Introduce Automation Gradually:** When the reliability of a system has not been sufficiently established, manual intervention and alternative workflows should remain available.
- **Keep Governance Relevant:** The discussion included Jaydeep's observation about excessive documentation associated with ISO and CMM processes. I understood that governance should contribute to quality and accountability instead of becoming an activity focused only on producing paperwork.
- **Create Reusable Products Instead of One-Customer Solutions:** Our professor questioned the suggestion that a vendor should be restricted from offering the same solution to other organisations. He recommended developing a common product foundation and accommodating customer-specific needs through configurations or separate extensions. This makes it possible to serve multiple customers without rebuilding the solution for every organisation.

**How I Intend to Apply This Learning at Work:** I have already observed situations in our chatbot-related work where identical prompts can generate different responses under different conditions. This lecture made me realise that simply checking whether an answer looks correct is not a sufficient evaluation method.

I want to establish a fixed collection of test scenarios and execute them whenever the underlying model, API or service provider changes. Comparing the outputs over time will help identify behavioural differences and provide a more objective basis for accepting changes before they reach production.

**My Personal Reflection:** My main learning was that product development should advance through demonstrated results rather than assumptions or pressure to release something quickly. Every stage, from the initial prototype to large-scale deployment, should provide enough evidence to justify the next investment.

I also realised that flexibility, transparency and systematic evaluation become especially important when a product depends on external AI services.

