## Class 9 – Establishing Business-Oriented Metrics and Reliable Evaluation Practices (10 October 2026)

This lecture concentrated on evaluating AI solutions and identifying performance indicators that genuinely represent business success.

The most interesting part for me was a practical Excel exercise in which our professor developed a loan payoff matrix. Through this example, he demonstrated why model accuracy alone is not sufficient to establish the commercial value of an AI solution.

The scenario involved a bank providing a loan of ₹1 lakh with a 2.5% spread. If a borrower defaulted, the bank would lose the entire principal amount.

By modifying the financial values associated with the four possible prediction outcomes, our professor demonstrated that a model achieving more than 90% accuracy could still result in a financial loss. At the same time, another model with comparatively lower accuracy could generate a profitable outcome.

The underlying reason was that the financial consequences of different prediction errors were not equal. One bad lending decision could eliminate the profit earned from approximately 40 successful loans.

This helped me understand that an accuracy percentage does not independently indicate whether an AI model is useful to a business. We must evaluate the financial implications of both correct and incorrect predictions.

During the discussion, Jaydeep suggested that recall would be an appropriate metric for this particular problem. However, our professor intentionally avoided presenting precision or recall as universally applicable measures.

Instead, he encouraged us to assign monetary values to each of the four possible outcomes, identify which stakeholder benefits or bears the loss and then evaluate the model based on its economic contribution.

I found both perspectives reasonable, but I personally found the financial approach particularly relevant to my MBA learning. Expressing model performance in monetary terms makes the discussion more practical and helps business stakeholders understand the actual value of investing in better model performance.

Other important concepts from the lecture included:

- **Consider Extreme Losses Alongside Average Results:** Our professor discussed how severe wildfires in California had contributed to significant financial damage for insurance companies. He also used a casino example to explain that probability calculations may assume an activity can continue indefinitely, but an individual or organisation can eventually run out of money. Businesses must therefore consider their ability to survive unusually large losses.
- **Review Metrics as the Product Develops:** A product may continue generating more revenue even while its new customer registrations decline. If the organisation continues monitoring the same indicators without reconsidering their relevance, important changes in customer behaviour may remain unnoticed. Metrics should evolve according to the product's current stage and objectives.
- **Understand Who Bears the Cost of Errors:** The financial responsibility associated with false positives and false negatives influences how seriously organisations address different types of mistakes. The banking example demonstrated how fraud prevention became a greater priority when regulatory requirements made banks accountable for the resulting financial consequences.
- **Avoid Creating Unhealthy Incentives Through Metrics:** If software testers are rewarded only according to the number of bugs they identify, they may be encouraged to delay reporting defects or focus on quantity rather than quality. This demonstrated that performance indicators should be evaluated not only for what they measure but also for the behaviour they encourage.
- **Apply Additional Caution When Conducting AI A/B Tests:** AI-based experiments can be difficult to interpret because external providers may simultaneously run their own experiments or introduce changes to model behaviour. An improvement in customer satisfaction from 4.5 to 4.8 should not automatically be considered proof of success. Sample size, statistical significance and other influencing factors must also be examined.

**An Area Where My Opinion Differs Slightly:** Our professor suggested limiting the number of performance indicators and initially concentrating on approximately three metrics. I agree that monitoring too many indicators can make decision-making unnecessarily complicated.

However, I believe that at least one of the selected indicators for an AI product should explicitly measure quality or safety. Otherwise, teams may concentrate primarily on reducing operational expenses and improving response speed while ignoring the reliability of the solution and the impact of incorrect responses.

**How I Intend to Apply This Learning at Work:** In the context of developing finance-related chatbots, I want to evaluate incorrect responses according to their practical consequences. Instead of presenting only an accuracy score, I would like to understand how an incorrect answer could affect a customer's financial decisions, time or money.

This would make performance reporting more meaningful to business stakeholders and help justify the investment required to improve the solution.

**My Personal Reflection:** This lecture significantly changed my understanding of AI evaluation. A model may perform impressively according to technical measurements but still fail to create meaningful financial benefits.

I realised that Product Managers must connect technical performance with business outcomes, understand the different costs associated with errors and select indicators that encourage responsible and useful behaviour.

