## Class 10 – Scaling AI Solutions While Maintaining the Customer Experience (17 October 2026)

The lecture started with an important clarification about the assessment process. Our professor explained that grading would follow a strict bell-curve distribution, the expected average would be approximately 70%, and submissions received after the deadline would not be considered.

The discussion then moved towards scaling AI-based products. One statement from our professor particularly stood out to me: **When teams focus only on infrastructure and forget the commitment made to customers, they are overlooking a critical product responsibility.**

He explained that every product establishes certain expectations among its customers. These expectations become even more significant when the product becomes an important part of their regular activities.

For example, WhatsApp does not require users to pay a subscription fee, yet customers can become extremely frustrated if the service is unavailable even for an hour. This demonstrates that customers evaluate a product based on the experience and dependability they expect from it rather than only considering what the organisation has formally guaranteed.

To manage these expectations, our professor introduced the **Customer Promise Matrix**. The organisation should identify the commitments it makes to different customer segments and establish suitable ways to measure whether those commitments are being fulfilled.

For example, if Uber creates an expectation that a cab will arrive within three minutes, measuring only server uptime will not indicate whether the organisation is meeting its actual customer promise. The product must be evaluated through the experience delivered to the customer.

The central topic of the lecture was the **Latency–Cost–Quality Trade-off**.

Our professor explained that determining the acceptable balance between these three dimensions is a Product Management responsibility.

For instance, a Product Manager may establish that a response must be delivered within 45 seconds, achieve a specified quality threshold and remain within an agreed operational cost.

If these expectations are not communicated clearly, different teams may make conflicting technical decisions. One engineer may prioritise response time and recommend a smaller model, while another may prioritise answer quality and prefer a more expensive model.

I understood that the Product Manager must establish clear boundaries so that individual technical decisions remain aligned with the overall product strategy.

Another interesting point was the importance of **predictability over maximum speed**. Our professor explained that allowing a few additional seconds for a final response-cleanup step may create a more consistent customer experience than trying to deliver every response as quickly as possible.

This reinforced the idea that the fastest solution is not necessarily the best solution from a customer's perspective.

Other important learnings included:

- **Maintain Versions of Everything That Affects Product Behaviour:** Versioning should extend beyond application code to include datasets, models, prompts and customer segments. Reverting an AI product to an earlier version may become difficult if the external provider has already discontinued that particular model.
- **Prepare for Rate Limits and Queuing Challenges:** These issues become increasingly important when customer adoption and request volumes grow. Organisations may require service tiers, fair-use policies and monitoring at the customer-segment level. Our professor used the example of Vande Bharat and regular passenger trains operating on the same railway track to explain the management of different service levels.
- **Control the Noisy Neighbour Problem:** In shared infrastructure, one customer consuming unusually large amounts of resources can negatively affect the performance available to other customers. This needs to be considered while designing multi-tenant products.
- **Make Thoughtful Decisions About Model Costs:** Smaller models should be preferred when they can deliver the required quality. Model routing and caching can also help reduce operating expenses. However, caching must be implemented carefully to ensure that one customer's information is never exposed through another customer's response.
- **Create Sustainable Team Structures:** Product development and scaling should not depend entirely on emergency efforts or a handful of individuals. Teams should have appropriate people assigned to different responsibilities. Our professor used the comparison between "commandos" and "army" personnel to explain why people should be selected according to the nature of the work.

**My Personal Connection:** The discussion about rate limits reminded me of a recent issue I encountered professionally. A system reached its maximum connection capacity and started rejecting new sessions instead of placing them in a waiting queue.

This helped me understand how two technically related situations can create very different customer experiences. An immediate rejection and a message asking someone to wait approximately 30 seconds may both result from resource limitations, but they communicate very different levels of service from a product perspective.

**Application of the Class Exercise:** Our professor asked us to identify the situations where smaller AI models would be adequate and where more advanced frontier models would be required.

I understood that smaller models are generally suitable for structured activities such as classification, request categorisation, field extraction and summaries with predefined formats.

However, more complex activities involving open-ended reasoning, multi-stage planning or significant legal and financial implications may require more capable models, along with suitable human review and additional safeguards.

**My Personal Reflection:** The biggest lesson from this lecture was that scaling is not simply a matter of increasing infrastructure capacity or supporting more requests. It is about ensuring that the customer continues receiving the experience originally promised by the product.

I also understood that decisions involving speed, quality and cost should be made deliberately at the product level instead of allowing different teams to optimise these factors independently.

Ultimately, a successful product must maintain consistent service quality even as its customer base, usage and operational complexity increase.

