## Class 11 – Developing Responsible AI Through Ethical Practices, Privacy and Governance (24 October 2026)

The lecture started with an introduction to our new laboratory, Prayogshala, along with an explanation of its intended purpose. Our professor clarified that it would primarily support traditional machine learning model development rather than agent-based product development or the integration of generative AI into applications.

The discussion then shifted towards important topics such as ethical AI, bias, privacy and governance.

One concept that I found particularly easy to understand was **Bias**.

Our professor explained it using an example of a child who has only seen Gurkha security guards and therefore develops the assumption that every security guard must belong to the same group. The child is not necessarily making a deliberate judgment. Instead, the conclusion is influenced by the limited experiences and information available to them.

AI systems can develop similar patterns when their training datasets do not adequately represent the diversity of situations and individuals they are expected to handle.

I understood that bias does not always originate from deliberate discrimination. It may enter a system through incomplete datasets, incorrect labelling, organisational policies or the processes used to collect and interpret information.

Recognising these possible sources is an important step towards building fairer AI applications.

When Gautam asked whether there was a universally accepted framework for addressing bias, our professor explained that no single framework could guarantee a complete solution. Organisations must follow applicable regulations and industry recommendations while continuously evaluating and improving their practices.

This is important because even established guidelines can sometimes contain outdated assumptions or reflect existing biases.

The next major subject was **Privacy and Responsible Data Collection**.

Our professor recommended collecting only information that the organisation can reasonably justify if questioned by a regulatory authority. For example, if a product does not require information such as a customer's age, gender or even name, there may be no reason to collect it.

I understood that avoiding unnecessary data collection also reduces the possibility of that information being misused later.

He further emphasised the need to communicate the purpose of data collection clearly and obtain appropriate consent. Particular care is required when dealing with sensitive information, including health-related data and information belonging to children.

We discussed India's DPDP framework and GDPR, both of which establish important requirements around the responsible collection and processing of personal information.

Our professor repeatedly reinforced the idea that organisations are custodians of customer data rather than its unrestricted owners. Requests for deletion and other data-related rights must be handled in accordance with the applicable legal requirements.

One statement from the lecture that I found particularly memorable was **"data is a drug"**. It described how organisations can develop an unnecessary habit of collecting increasing amounts of information even when they cannot clearly explain why they need it.

I realised that responsible product development requires discipline not only in deciding how data will be used but also in determining what information should never be collected.

We also discussed practical methods for reducing **AI Hallucinations**. These included:

- **Grounding Through RAG:** Connecting the AI system to relevant and trustworthy information sources so that its responses can be supported by available evidence.
- **Restricting the Available Output Space:** Limiting the choices available to the model when a task requires a controlled set of possible responses.
- **Answer, Abstain or Escalate:** Allowing the system to respond when it has sufficient confidence, refrain from answering when the available information is inadequate or transfer the matter to a human for further evaluation.

I found the third approach particularly valuable because it changes the expectation that an AI system must always produce an answer.

In some situations, refusing to provide an unreliable response is the most responsible action. It can also be reflected in the commercial model, where a customer may not be charged when the system fails to provide a useful result.

The discussion on **Governance** introduced two important principles:

- Governance practices must develop quickly enough to remain effective as the number of AI agents and applications increases.
- Committees can review proposals and provide recommendations, but the final responsibility remains with the Product Owner.

Our professor also cautioned against implementing governance merely for the purpose of creating documentation. If reviewers cannot explain the actual benefit of a review activity, it risks becoming an example of governance theatre.

Another important point was that people responsible for reviewing AI systems should receive adequate time and recognition. If such responsibilities are treated as additional work without sufficient support, reviewers may approve submissions without examining them properly.

Under **Risk Assessment**, I learned that probability and severity are not the only factors that should be considered. We must also evaluate whether a potential failure can be detected and whether its consequences can be reversed.

The importance of preparing an incident communication plan was also discussed. Our professor shared an example of informing people that they were safe if they had not received a particular email by 7 a.m. This illustrated how well-planned communication can prevent confusion and unnecessary panic during critical situations.

**Application to Our Project:** We were instructed to create a dedicated section explaining the information our product collects, the process through which consent is obtained, the retention period and the regulatory requirements applicable to the relevant industry.

We were also expected to develop a launch-readiness checklist that clearly distinguishes between essential conditions and issues that would prevent the product from being released.

The objective was to make these considerations specific to our proposed solution rather than relying on generic statements about ethical AI.

**My Personal Reflection:** One relatively small but useful observation from this session was our professor's repeated advice to avoid using the generic term *user* without specifying the actual stakeholder.

I started noticing how frequently I use expressions such as "business users" in my own professional documentation. In reality, I should identify whether I am referring to a finance executive, an administrator, a customer-support representative or another specific role.

Overall, this lecture helped me understand that responsible AI involves much more than preventing bias or meeting regulatory requirements. It requires continuous attention to data collection, transparency, accountability, error management and risk throughout the product lifecycle.

Good governance should protect both customers and the organisation without introducing unnecessary barriers to innovation.

