## Class 12 – Improving Product Documentation, Architecture Visualisation and Commercial Planning (31 October 2026)

The lecture began with a practical review of one team's project submission. Although the professor's feedback was initially directed towards a particular group, I found the discussion useful because many of the observations could also be applied to our own project.

One of the strongest messages was that a **Product Requirements Document (PRD) should be prepared with the same level of seriousness as a contractual document being shared with an external vendor**, particularly when the organisation is investing its own money in the proposed solution.

Our professor explained that expressions such as "accurate", "user-friendly", "time reduction", "unsafe output" and "high risk" are too broad to communicate precise expectations. Different vendors may interpret these terms differently, resulting in a solution that does not meet the actual requirement.

He recommended replacing vague descriptions with clearly measurable expectations. For example, instead of simply asking for reduced processing time, we should define a measurable objective such as a 30% reduction.

He also suggested maintaining a separate column for target values so that numerical expectations can be modified later without rewriting the original requirement.

Another important observation was to avoid building unnecessary restrictions into the product through fixed assumptions. For instance, if an application is designed to support only 12 medicines, it may become difficult to accommodate additional medicines in the future.

Similarly, important workflows should be described clearly from the beginning, even if certain implementation details are expected to evolve during the pilot stage.

The PRD must also explain precisely where AI is involved in the proposed solution. For every AI-driven activity, the team should identify expected reliability, operational expenses, uncertainty handling and the possibility of human intervention.

I understood that simply mentioning that a product uses AI does not explain how the technology contributes to its functionality. Its specific responsibilities and limitations must be documented.

One recommendation from our professor that I found particularly practical was to **use an AI system to review the PRD and determine whether it contains sufficient information to build the proposed product**.

We should also ask the system to identify missing details and list the questions that would need clarification before development could begin.

If the document leaves an AI system with several unanswered questions, an external development vendor is likely to face similar difficulties. This provides an opportunity to identify documentation gaps before development starts.

The lecture also introduced two useful tools for creating technical diagrams: **PlantUML and Mermaid**. Both allow diagrams to be generated through text-based descriptions.

We discussed swimlane diagrams, where individual lanes represent different participants or system components, such as a patient, doctor, application or AI component. These diagrams help explain the sequence of activities, responsibilities and interactions across a workflow.

I found the ability to maintain diagrams as text particularly beneficial because they can be stored in Git repositories and compared through version-control changes.

Our professor also introduced the **C4 Model**, which provides a structured approach to visualising software architecture. He explained that Product Managers generally need to understand the system context and container levels rather than becoming involved in every low-level technical implementation detail.

Another useful suggestion was to prepare project documentation in Markdown because it can be processed more easily by AI systems, while also maintaining PDF versions for human readers.

This highlighted the importance of presenting information in formats that are suitable for different audiences and tools.

The second half of the lecture focused on **Monetization and Product Economics**. My major takeaways were:

- **Target Costing:** The Japanese target-costing approach begins with identifying the price customers are willing to pay. The desired profit margin is then deducted, and the remaining amount becomes the maximum acceptable cost of producing and delivering the product. This is different from calculating the cost first and adding profit afterwards.
- **Customers Purchase Results While Vendors Manage Resources:** Customers generally care about the outcomes they receive, whereas vendors are responsible for managing the resources required to deliver those outcomes. Customers may prefer outcome-linked pricing, while vendors often favour arrangements such as Time and Material (T&M). A provider capable of managing delivery-related risks effectively may have an advantage in winning contracts.
- **Consider Inference Expenses at Scale:** Increased product adoption can result in higher usage and, consequently, greater AI inference expenses. Pricing models should therefore be evaluated against high-volume scenarios rather than relying exclusively on average consumption.
- **Provide Flexible Options for Low-Usage Customers:** Customers with limited usage should not automatically be considered unprofitable. Offering a lower-priced subscription or downgrade option may help retain such customers instead of losing the relationship entirely.
- **Connect Architecture With Financial Considerations:** Our professor used examples involving Oracle licensing to explain how commercial agreements can influence technical design. In one scenario, database requests were routed through a common module because licensing depended on the number of connections. In another, databases were consolidated because licensing expenses were linked to CPU cores. These examples showed that architectural decisions must account for financial implications alongside technical requirements.
- **Understand Unit Economics and Contribution Margin:** Product profitability should be analysed across individual customer segments and workflow types. A Product Manager should be capable of explaining the economics of the product clearly, even within a short conversation with a potential investor.
- **Platforms Benefit From Network Effects:** A platform can become increasingly valuable as more participants join its ecosystem. Its competitive advantage may come from the relationships and interactions among participants rather than only from its individual features.

**My Personal Connection:** The discussion about Oracle licensing was especially relatable because I have encountered similar situations while working with data platforms. Licensing expenses and connection restrictions can directly influence the way database integrations and access mechanisms are designed.

I have seen situations where financial constraints become more important than technical preferences when making architecture decisions. This helped me connect the professor's discussion about product economics with challenges I have observed in actual technology environments.

**My Personal Reflection:** This lecture helped me understand that successful Product Management requires attention to documentation, technical architecture and financial sustainability at the same time.

A well-defined PRD reduces ambiguity, diagrams improve communication and monetization decisions determine whether the product can operate sustainably.

The key learning for me was that solving a customer problem is only one part of product success. The way the solution is designed, delivered and priced must also make commercial sense.

